'''Autopoiesis''' is a theory of living organization developed by Humberto Maturana and Francisco Varela. It describes a system whose network of processes produces and maintains the components and boundary that make that same network possible.

== Original formulation ==
Varela, Maturana and Uribe's 1974 article characterizes living systems as networks of component production whose interactions continually realize the organization and its boundary. Maturana's subsequent account emphasizes organizational closure: the processes constituting the unity are recursively enabled by the network. Closure in this organizational sense does not mean thermodynamic closure, which would prevent ordinary metabolic exchange.

== Mechanistic representation ==
Represent components C and processes R as a directed bipartite production graph. A process consumes or transforms components, while produced components may support other processes or maintain an enclosing boundary. In an illustrative chemical model <math>\dot c=S v(c)+J(c)</math>, S is a stoichiometric matrix, v represents reaction rates and J describes boundary exchange. A positive stable concentration is not sufficient for autopoiesis: the organization must also recursively produce the constraints and boundary enabling the relevant processes. Explicitly distinguish externally supplied catalysts or enclosure from components maintained by the network itself.

== Empirical and computational questions ==
A protocell study can test whether membrane components are generated internally, whether transport supports the reactions, and whether the network recovers from a bounded perturbation without external reconstruction. A cellular-automaton model may demonstrate formal self-maintenance under its rules; it does not establish biochemical viability. Self-organization and homeostasis are related but not identical: a stable vortex can organize itself without producing an organism's component network. Extensions to social systems and AI require specifying the components, process closure and boundary independently; metaphorical use is not evidence of biological life.

